## Prerequisites

- Kubernetes 1.24+
- Helm 3.0.0+
- [metrics-server](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server) installed in the cluster (required for resource metrics collection)

## Get Repo Info

```console
helm repo add kubeadapt https://kubeadapt.github.io/kubeadapt-helm
helm repo update
```

## Installing the Chart

Recommended: keep the agent token in a Secret, so it never appears on a command line or in `helm get values`.

```console
kubectl create namespace kubeadapt
kubectl create secret generic kubeadapt-agent-token --namespace kubeadapt --from-literal=token='<your-token>'
helm install kubeadapt kubeadapt/{{ .Name }} --namespace kubeadapt --set agent.config.existingSecret=kubeadapt-agent-token
```

The agent pod meets the Kubernetes restricted Pod Security Standard by default. On OpenShift, add
`--set agent.podSecurityContext.runAsUser=null --set agent.podSecurityContext.runAsGroup=null --set agent.podSecurityContext.fsGroup=null`
so the restricted-v2 SCC can assign the user and group.

## Network Monitoring

The network monitor (`kubeadapt-k8s-pulse`) is off by default. `--set kubeadapt-k8s-pulse.enabled=true` deploys its DaemonSet, turns on the agent's listener for it behind the `<name>-agent-pulse` ClusterIP Service, and gives both sides the same token: a Secret the chart creates once and keeps across upgrades, or your own Secret named in `global.pulse.existingSecret` (key `token`). Use your own Secret when a tool renders the chart without cluster access, such as Argo CD.

The DaemonSet needs Linux 5.10 or newer, the node's network namespace, read-only node paths, writable `/sys/fs/bpf` and the capabilities `BPF`, `PERFMON`, `NET_ADMIN`, `SYS_RESOURCE` and `SYS_PTRACE`, so its namespace must allow the privileged Pod Security level. See the [kubeadapt-k8s-pulse chart](../kubeadapt-k8s-pulse) for each requirement, for scheduling, rollout and private registries, and for troubleshooting; [examples/network-monitor](../../examples/network-monitor) has policy exceptions.

It runs on every Linux node except AWS Fargate and virtual-kubelet nodes, tolerates every taint and never preempts a workload. In a namespace that denies ingress by default, set `agent.networkPolicy.enabled=true` and `agent.networkPolicy.nodeCIDRs` so the DaemonSet, which connects from node addresses, can reach the agent.

## Upgrading

```console
helm upgrade kubeadapt kubeadapt/{{ .Name }} --namespace kubeadapt --reset-then-reuse-values
```

`--reset-then-reuse-values` (Helm 3.14 or newer) keeps your settings and takes everything else from the new chart. `--reuse-values` also keeps the previous version's defaults, image tags included: with the network monitor off the chart renders and notes it, with `kubeadapt-k8s-pulse.enabled=true` it stops and asks for `--reset-then-reuse-values`.

## Uninstalling the Chart

```console
helm uninstall kubeadapt --namespace kubeadapt
```

The namespace and any token Secret you created yourself stay behind; delete them with `kubectl` if you no longer need them. Then delete the cluster on the **Clusters** page in Kubeadapt so its token stops working.

